1 LOAD
    The LOAD command reads the contents of a saved memory image file
    into the current virtual VAX.  The LOAD command restores the contents
    of memory and the registers.  It does not restore saved symbols,
    breakpoints, or other console-specific context.   Also see the help
    for the LOAD/ROM command is used to load a ROM image into the console
    ROM memory, and LOAD/NVRAM to load non-volatile RAM images.

    Syntax:

        LOAD    <file-name>



    Parameter:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "Saved.VAX"
                        is assumed.
2 /NVRAM
    The LOAD/NVRAM command reads a non-volatile RAM file into the special
    memory area in the virtual VAX physical address space for the NVRAM.
    The NVRAM area exists on some VAX systems to contain console boot data,
    etc. and is used by the console ROM on those systems.  The NVRAM file
    is created with the SAVE/NVRAM command after INIT/NVRAM has first
    allocated it.


    Syntax:


        LOAD/NVRAM  <filename>

            or

        LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR


    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.nvram"
                        is assumed.  The file is found as LOAD/ROM's
                        is: a volume file or a host file.


        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default NVRAM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a NVRAM file is not found.
2 /ROM
    The LOAD/ROM command reads a ROM file into the special memory area
    in the virtual VAX physical address space for a console ROM.  This
    ROM file can be a text file containing the console EXAM command
    output of the console memory, or a binary image created with the
    SAVE/ROM command.

    Syntax:


        LOAD/ROM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>] <filename>

            or

        LOAD/ROM/NOERROR


    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.rom"
                        is assumed.  A VMS file specification
                        (SYS$SYSTEM:DEFAULT.ROM) is a file on a
                        mounted volume, a host path a host file, and
                        a bare name a file in the default directory
                        when SET DEFAULT is on a volume.  A bare name
                        that isn't there, or any name when the default
                        is on the host, is looked for in the host's
                        current directory, the --path directories,
                        and govax's built-in files.  STARTUP.COM loads
                        the system volume's SYS$SYSTEM:DEFAULT.ROM.

        <base-addr>     When loading a ROM image, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the ROM image
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20040000 is assumed.

        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the ROM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is ^d256 for a 256K ROM.

        Note that /BASE and /SIZE cannot be used if the ROM file being
        read is a ROM image created by SAVE/ROM; this has a fixed address
        and size associated with it.

        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default ROM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/ROM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a ROM file is not found.
